window.AILETMOCK={"n":3,"title":"Mock Test 3","theme":"Elections, privilege and reservations","kind":"Full-length paper","blurb":"Free and fair elections, the Emergency, Sabarimala, parliamentary privilege and reservation in promotions in constitutional law. Seven more branches of law and contemporary legal GK complete the paper.","minutes":120,"neg":0.25,"sec":["Constitutional Law","Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","International Law and Human Rights","Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK"],"q":[{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Free and Fair Elections","q":"Which decision held 'free and fair elections' to be part of the basic structure and struck down clause (4) of Article 329A inserted by the 39th Amendment?","o":["Minerva Mills Ltd. v. Union of India (1980)","Union of India v. Association for Democratic Reforms (2002)","Indira Nehru Gandhi v. Raj Narain (1975)","Kihoto Hollohan v. Zachillhu (1992)"],"a":2,"e":"A five-judge bench applied the basic structure doctrine to invalidate a provision that sought to validate the Prime Minister's election retrospectively, holding it violated democracy, equality and the rule of law."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Emergency","q":"The decision that, during the Emergency, held that the right to move courts for habeas corpus stood suspended, later described as wrongly decided in Puttaswamy (2017), is:","o":["A.K. Gopalan v. State of Madras (1950)","Kharak Singh v. State of Uttar Pradesh (1962)","Maneka Gandhi v. Union of India (1978)","A.D.M. Jabalpur v. Shivkant Shukla (1976)"],"a":3,"e":"By a 4:1 majority, with Justice H.R. Khanna dissenting, the Court held that Article 21 could not be enforced during the Emergency. The Constitution (44th Amendment) later made Articles 20 and 21 non-suspendable."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Religious Freedom","q":"In Aishat Shifa v. State of Karnataka (2022), the Supreme Court, on the question of the hijab ban in classrooms, delivered:","o":["a unanimous verdict upholding the ban in pre-university colleges","a verdict dismissing the appeals as premature and infructuous","a unanimous verdict striking down the ban on all uniforms","a split verdict, leading to a reference to a larger bench"],"a":3,"e":"The two judges differed on the essential religious practices test and on the nature of the right, so the matter was sent to the Chief Justice for constitution of an appropriate bench."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Free Speech","q":"Which ground for restricting free speech under Article 19(2) was added by the First Amendment, 1951?","o":["Sovereignty and integrity of India","Public order","Security of the State","Decency or morality"],"a":1,"e":"The First Amendment added 'public order', 'friendly relations with foreign States' and 'incitement to an offence'. 'Sovereignty and integrity of India' came through the 16th Amendment (1963), while the other two were original."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Right to Education","q":"Under Section 12(1)(c) of the Right of Children to Free and Compulsory Education Act, 2009, private unaided schools must reserve what proportion of entry-level seats for children from weaker sections?","o":["15 per cent","10 per cent","25 per cent","33 per cent"],"a":2,"e":"Pramati Educational and Cultural Trust v. Union of India (2014) upheld the 25% quota as applicable to non-minority unaided schools, while exempting minority institutions."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Sabarimala","q":"In Indian Young Lawyers Association v. State of Kerala (2018), the majority held that the exclusion of women of menstruating age from the Sabarimala temple:","o":["was valid, since courts cannot test any religious practice","was valid, as it concerns a temple run by a private trust","was protected as a denominational right under Article 26(b)","violated Article 25(1); it was not an essential practice"],"a":3,"e":"The 4:1 judgment (Justice Indu Malhotra dissenting) was followed by a review reference raising wider questions on the scope of essential religious practices, which a larger bench has been examining."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Parliamentary Privilege","q":"In Sita Soren v. Union of India (2024), a seven-judge bench held that legislators:","o":["are immune from prosecution for a bribe taken for a vote, under Article 105(2)","enjoy no immunity from prosecution for bribery tied to vote or speech","are immune from such prosecution, but only if they voted as promised","are immune from prosecution for any crime committed inside the House"],"a":1,"e":"The Court overruled P.V. Narasimha Rao v. State (1998), reasoning that bribery is not essential to the performance of a legislator's duty and that corruption erodes parliamentary democracy."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Judges","q":"Under Article 124(4), a Supreme Court judge can be removed by the President on an address by each House of Parliament supported by:","o":["a majority of total membership and two-thirds of members present and voting","two-thirds of the total membership of each of the two Houses of Parliament","a simple majority of members present and voting in each of the Houses","a majority of the total membership of the Lok Sabha alone, by resolution"],"a":0,"e":"Removal is only for proved misbehaviour or incapacity, following the procedure of the Judges (Inquiry) Act, 1968. No judge has yet been removed."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Executive","q":"Under Article 75(1A), inserted by the 91st Amendment (2003), the total number of Ministers including the Prime Minister cannot exceed what proportion of the Lok Sabha's strength?","o":["20 per cent","25 per cent","15 per cent","10 per cent"],"a":2,"e":"The same ceiling applies to State Councils of Ministers (15% of the Assembly), subject to a minimum of 12 Ministers. The amendment also tightened anti-defection provisions."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Federalism","q":"Which Article provides for the adjudication of disputes relating to waters of inter-State rivers or river valleys?","o":["Article 263","Article 262","Article 131","Article 280"],"a":1,"e":"Article 262 empowers Parliament to bar the jurisdiction of courts in such disputes, which it did in the Inter-State River Water Disputes Act, 1956. Article 263 provides for the Inter-State Council."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Speedy Trial","q":"The recognition of the right to speedy trial as part of Article 21 first arose in:","o":["Maneka Gandhi v. Union of India (1978)","Mohini Jain v. State of Karnataka (1992)","Sunil Batra v. Delhi Administration (1978)","Hussainara Khatoon v. State of Bihar (1979)"],"a":3,"e":"The case concerned thousands of undertrial prisoners in Bihar jails who had been held for periods longer than the maximum sentence for their offences; it also led to the recognition of free legal aid as a right."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Reservation in Promotions","q":"In M. Nagaraj v. Union of India (2006), the Supreme Court upheld Article 16(4A) subject to the State collecting:","o":["a recommendation from the National Commission for SCs in each case","quantifiable data on backwardness and inadequacy of representation","a two-thirds majority of the State legislature for each promotion","prior judicial sanction from the High Court for each promotion"],"a":1,"e":"The State must also ensure that the overall efficiency of administration under Article 335 is not compromised. Jarnail Singh v. Lachhmi Narain Gupta (2018) later dispensed with the creamy layer requirement for SCs and STs in promotions."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Governors","q":"In Subhash Desai v. Principal Secretary, Governor of Maharashtra (2023), the Constitution Bench held that:","o":["the Speaker's choice of Whip is final and cannot be examined","the Governor had no objective material to call a floor test","a Governor may always call a floor test on the strength of a party split","the floor test is a purely political question beyond judicial review"],"a":1,"e":"The Court nevertheless declined to restore the earlier Chief Minister, since he had resigned without facing the floor test."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Delhi","q":"In Government of NCT of Delhi v. Union of India (2023), the Constitution Bench held that the elected Delhi Government has legislative and executive power over services, excluding:","o":["police, public order and land","revenue, transport and local government","finance, education and health","police, defence and foreign affairs"],"a":0,"e":"Parliament responded with the Government of NCT of Delhi (Amendment) Act, 2023, which created a National Capital Civil Service Authority, and the validity of that Act is under challenge."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Tribal Areas","q":"The Sixth Schedule, providing autonomous district councils, applies to tribal areas in:","o":["Assam, Manipur, Nagaland and Mizoram","Assam, Meghalaya, Tripura and Mizoram","Assam, Arunachal Pradesh, Manipur and Tripura","Meghalaya, Nagaland, Tripura and Sikkim"],"a":1,"e":"The Sixth Schedule is under Articles 244(2) and 275(1). The demand to extend it to Ladakh is a continuing political and legal issue."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Election Commission","q":"Under the Chief Election Commissioner and Other Election Commissioners (Appointment, Conditions of Service and Term of Office) Act, 2023, the selection committee consists of:","o":["the Prime Minister, the Home Minister, the Chief Justice of India and the Vice-President","the President, the Prime Minister, the Speaker of the Lok Sabha and the Law Minister","the Prime Minister, the Chief Justice of India and the Leader of the Opposition","the Prime Minister, a Cabinet Minister nominated by him, and the Leader of Opposition"],"a":3,"e":"Anoop Baranwal v. Union of India (2023) had directed, pending legislation, a panel with the CJI. The 2023 Act replaced the CJI with a Cabinet Minister, and has been challenged."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Death Penalty","q":"Shatrughan Chauhan v. Union of India (2014) held that undue and unexplained delay by the executive in deciding a mercy petition is a ground for:","o":["directing a fresh trial before a different Sessions Judge","setting aside the conviction and acquitting the accused","commuting the death sentence to life imprisonment","declaring the death penalty unconstitutional per se"],"a":2,"e":"The Court read Article 21 as protecting even death row convicts against inordinate delay and inhuman treatment, while reiterating that the death penalty itself remains valid under Bachan Singh."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Pardon","q":"Unlike the Governor's power under Article 161, the President's pardoning power under Article 72 extends to:","o":["sentences by court-martial and to sentences of death","only sentences of fine and not of imprisonment","only sentences imposed by High Courts","only sentences under State laws on subjects in the State List"],"a":0,"e":"The Governor can suspend, remit or commute a death sentence but cannot pardon it, and has no power over court-martial sentences."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Doctrine of Eclipse","q":"The doctrine of eclipse, laid down in Bhikaji Narain Dhakras v. State of M.P. (1955), applies to:","o":["post-constitutional laws that violate fundamental rights","laws that are beyond the legislative competence of a State","pre-constitutional laws inconsistent with fundamental rights","ordinances that have lapsed after six weeks of Parliament's reassembly"],"a":2,"e":"Such a law is not void ab initio but only unenforceable (eclipsed), and revives if the inconsistency is removed. Post-constitutional laws violating Part III are void from the start (Deep Chand v. State of U.P.)."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Legislative Competence","q":"The doctrine of colourable legislation, expressed by the idea that what cannot be done directly cannot be done indirectly, was applied in:","o":["K.C. Gajapati Narayan Deo v. State of Orissa (1953)","State of Bihar v. Maharajadhiraja Kameshwar Singh (1952)","A.K. Roy v. Union of India (Preventive Detention case, 1982)","Ram Krishna Dalmia v. Justice S.R. Tendolkar (1958)"],"a":0,"e":"The doctrine concerns the competence of the legislature, not its motive. A legislature that lacks power over a subject cannot achieve the result by disguising the law as one on another subject."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Right to Livelihood","q":"In Olga Tellis v. Bombay Municipal Corporation (1985), the Supreme Court held that the right to life under Article 21 includes:","o":["the right to employment, so the State must provide a government job to all","the right to a house, so pavement dwellers can never be evicted in any manner","the right to property, so every eviction requires full market-value compensation","the right to livelihood, so pavement dwellers need fair process before eviction"],"a":3,"e":"The Court held that deprivation of livelihood would deprive life itself, but permitted eviction after notice and an opportunity to be heard and with alternative accommodation where possible."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Cooperative Societies","q":"In Union of India v. Rajendra N. Shah (2021), the Supreme Court struck down Part IXB of the Constitution (97th Amendment) insofar as it applied to:","o":["cooperative societies governed by State laws, for want of ratification by States","cooperative banks regulated by the Reserve Bank of India under the BR Act","all cooperative societies, including those functioning in the Union Territories","multi-State cooperative societies, for want of Parliament's legislative competence"],"a":0,"e":"Since cooperative societies is a State subject (Entry 32, List II), the amendment affecting it required ratification by half the States under Article 368(2), which was not obtained. The part survives for multi-State societies."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Arrest","q":"In Pankaj Bansal v. Union of India (2023), the Supreme Court held that, under Article 22(1), the grounds of arrest must be:","o":["shown to the Magistrate on request after the first remand","disclosed only after the filing of the charge-sheet","communicated orally to the arrested person's relatives only","furnished to the arrested person in writing"],"a":3,"e":"Though decided under the PMLA, the Court in later decisions has extended the written communication requirement to other offences. Non-compliance can vitiate the arrest and subsequent remand."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Horizontal Rights","q":"In Kaushal Kishore v. State of U.P. (2023), the Constitution Bench held that, as regards speech by public functionaries:","o":["no restrictions beyond Article 19(2) can be added, though Article 21 may operate against private actors","the State is always vicariously liable for every statement made by its Ministers in public","additional restrictions beyond Article 19(2) may be imposed through guidelines issued by courts","fundamental rights can never be enforced against private persons in any circumstance of law"],"a":0,"e":"The Court held that grounds in Article 19(2) are exhaustive and that a Minister's statement cannot, in general, be attributed to the Government, though a statement made in an official capacity may attract vicarious liability."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"Under the BNS, the punishment for rape under Section 64(1) is rigorous imprisonment for a term:","o":["not less than 14 years, which may extend to death, and fine","not less than 10 years, which may extend to life, and fine","not less than 7 years, which may extend to 10 years, and fine","not less than 5 years, which may extend to 10 years, and fine"],"a":1,"e":"Section 63 defines rape, and Section 64 prescribes the punishment, with enhanced punishment for aggravated forms in sub-section (2). Section 70 covers gang rape."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"The BNS provision corresponding to Section 498A of the IPC (cruelty by husband or his relatives) is Section:","o":["85","84","80","87"],"a":0,"e":"Section 85 punishes the cruelty, and Section 86 defines it. Section 80 is dowry death, and Section 84 is enticing a married woman."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"Under Section 80 of the BNS, dowry death is punishable with imprisonment of:","o":["life imprisonment or death, with no lesser punishment","not less than 3 years, which may extend to 10 years","not less than 7 years, which may extend to life imprisonment","not less than 10 years, which may extend to life imprisonment"],"a":2,"e":"The offence applies where a woman's death occurs within seven years of marriage in unnatural circumstances, preceded by cruelty or harassment for dowry. The BSA contains the related presumption."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"The offence of cheating is defined in the BNS under Section:","o":["308","319","318","316"],"a":2,"e":"Section 318 covers cheating, 316 criminal breach of trust, 319 cheating by personation and 308 extortion."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNSS","q":"Section 105 of the BNSS mandates audio-video recording of:","o":["the examination of a witness during an open trial","the deliberations of judges while preparing a judgment","the proceedings of all bail hearings before the Magistrate","the process of search and seizure"],"a":3,"e":"The recording must be made by the police during search and seizure and forwarded to the Magistrate without delay, as a safeguard against fabricated recoveries."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNSS","q":"Under Section 176(3) of the BNSS, a forensic expert must visit the crime scene to collect evidence for offences punishable with imprisonment of at least:","o":["ten years","five years","seven years","three years"],"a":2,"e":"States were allowed a transition period to build forensic capacity. The provision aims to improve conviction quality through scientific evidence."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"Evidence","q":"Which of the following is correct about a dying declaration under Indian evidence law?","o":["It is admissible only if the maker dies within twenty-four hours","It is admissible only if corroborated by an eyewitness account","It is admissible even if its maker did not expect death at the time","It is admissible only if recorded by a Magistrate in all cases"],"a":2,"e":"Unlike English law, the Indian provision (now Section 26(1) of the BSA) does not require expectation of death (Khushal Rao v. State of Bombay). A reliable declaration can sustain a conviction without corroboration."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"Evidence","q":"When an accused pleads a general exception, the burden of proving that the case falls within it lies on:","o":["the prosecution, who must disprove it beyond reasonable doubt in every case","the court, which must independently investigate the plea of the accused","the accused, who must prove it beyond all reasonable doubt in every case","the accused, who must prove it only on a preponderance of probability"],"a":3,"e":"The accused need not prove the exception beyond reasonable doubt, and may discharge the burden by showing a reasonable probability. The prosecution still retains the burden on all ingredients of the offence."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"Circumstantial Evidence","q":"The 'five golden principles' (panchsheel) for conviction on circumstantial evidence were laid down in:","o":["Virsa Singh v. State of Punjab (murder intention test, 1958)","Bachan Singh v. State of Punjab (Rarest of rare case, 1980)","Tukaram v. State of Maharashtra (Mathura rape case, 1979)","Sharad Birdhichand Sarda v. State of Maharashtra (1984)"],"a":3,"e":"The circumstances must be fully established, consistent only with the guilt of the accused, conclusive in nature, and must exclude every hypothesis except that of guilt."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"Does the BNS contain a standalone offence of attempt to commit suicide?","o":["No, save Section 226 on compelling a public servant by threat of suicide","Yes, but only when the person is a government servant or a student","Yes, in the same form as Section 309 of the old Indian Penal Code, 1860","No, and no other provision of the BNS deals with suicide in any manner"],"a":0,"e":"Section 309 of the IPC was not carried forward, in line with the Mental Healthcare Act, 2017, which presumes that a person attempting suicide is under severe stress and not punishable."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"Under Section 111 of the BNS, organised crime that results in the death of any person is punishable with:","o":["imprisonment for ten years only, and fine up to ₹5 lakh at most","life imprisonment only, and no fine is prescribed for this offence","imprisonment for five to ten years, and fine of not less than ₹1 lakh","death or life imprisonment, and fine of not less than ₹10 lakh"],"a":3,"e":"Section 111 consolidates offences such as kidnapping, extortion and cyber-crimes committed by organised crime syndicates, an area earlier addressed by State laws such as MCOCA."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNSS","q":"Under Section 173(3) of the BNSS, for a cognizable offence punishable with three years or more but less than seven, the police may, with permission of a DSP-level officer, conduct a preliminary enquiry within:","o":["14 days","30 days","21 days","7 days"],"a":0,"e":"The enquiry is meant to ascertain whether a prima facie case exists. It supplements Lalita Kumari v. Government of U.P. (2013), which required registration of FIR for cognizable offences, with limited preliminary enquiry in specified categories."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNSS","q":"Under Section 193(3)(ii) of the BNSS, the police must inform the informant or victim of the progress of the investigation within:","o":["120 days","60 days","90 days","30 days"],"a":2,"e":"The communication may be by electronic means. The provision seeks to enhance transparency and victim participation."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BSA","q":"The certificate accompanying an electronic record under Section 63(4) of the BSA must include the hash value and be signed by:","o":["a Magistrate and a senior gazetted police officer","the complainant and the officer investigating the case","the accused person and the advocate appearing for him","the person in charge of the device and an expert"],"a":3,"e":"Unlike old Section 65B, the BSA requires a two-part certificate prescribed in the Schedule, in which the expert's signature is also needed, and hash values to verify integrity."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Contract","q":"A, a spiritual guru, persuades his devoted disciple B to gift him valuable property. B later challenges the gift. The contract is:","o":["voidable for undue influence; the burden is on A to prove no influence","voidable, with the burden lying on B to prove that influence existed","void ab initio, since A did not pay any consideration to the disciple","valid, since the gift was made by the disciple of his own free will"],"a":0,"e":"Under Section 16, where one party is in a position to dominate the will of the other and obtains an unfair advantage, the transaction is presumed to be induced by undue influence, and the dominating party must rebut it."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Fraud","q":"In Derry v. Peek (1889), fraud was defined as a false statement made:","o":["innocently, but without taking reasonable care in making the statement","knowingly, without belief in its truth, or recklessly as to its truth","negligently, with the intention of obtaining profit from the statement","honestly, but on the basis of information that turned out to be wrong"],"a":1,"e":"A false statement made honestly, however careless, is not fraud. Section 17 of the Indian Contract Act adopts a similar approach to fraud and separates it from innocent misrepresentation under Section 18."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Mistake","q":"Under Section 20 of the Indian Contract Act, where both parties are under a mistake as to a matter of fact essential to the agreement, the agreement is:","o":["voidable at the option of either party","void","valid, if the mistake concerns a matter of law","valid, but liable to be modified by court"],"a":1,"e":"Under Section 21, a mistake as to a law in force in India does not make the contract voidable; a mistake of foreign law is treated as a mistake of fact."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Quasi-Contract","q":"Under Section 70 of the Indian Contract Act, where a person lawfully does something for another non-gratuitously and the other enjoys the benefit, the latter is:","o":["bound to compensate the former for the thing done or delivered","not liable, since there was no prior promise made to pay anything","liable only to return the thing, without paying any compensation","bound to compensate only if a written contract exists between them"],"a":0,"e":"State of West Bengal v. B.K. Mondal & Sons (1962) held that Section 70 applies even to a government, where the work was done lawfully and non-gratuitously and the benefit was accepted."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Guarantee","q":"Under Section 128 of the Indian Contract Act, the liability of the surety is:","o":["co-extensive with the debtor's liability, unless the contract says otherwise","limited to one-half of the principal debtor's liability, as a default rule","independent of the principal debtor's liability, in every case without exception","secondary, and arises only after the creditor first sues the principal debtor"],"a":0,"e":"The creditor may proceed against the surety without first suing the principal debtor, unless the contract provides otherwise."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Bailment","q":"Under Section 151 of the Indian Contract Act, a bailee must take as much care of the goods bailed as:","o":["a trustee must take of trust property held for a beneficiary's benefit","a professional warehouse-keeper takes of goods belonging to a stranger","a borrower takes of an article that was lent to him for his own use","a man of ordinary prudence would take of his own goods of the same kind"],"a":3,"e":"Under Section 152, in the absence of a special contract, the bailee is not liable for loss, destruction or deterioration of the goods if he has taken such care."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Sale of Goods","q":"Under the Sale of Goods Act, 1930, breach of a condition by the seller entitles the buyer to:","o":["claim a refund of the price, without any damages","specific performance only, and not repudiate the contract","repudiate the contract, and also claim damages","claim damages only, and not repudiate the contract"],"a":2,"e":"A breach of warranty gives rise only to a claim for damages. A condition may be treated as a warranty if the buyer elects to do so or accepts the goods."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Torts","q":"Which of the following is NOT an essential element of the tort of malicious prosecution?","o":["The proceeding was initiated without reasonable and probable cause","The defendant acted with malice or an improper motive","The proceeding terminated in the plaintiff's favour","The plaintiff must have been convicted in the proceeding"],"a":3,"e":"The plaintiff must show that he was prosecuted by the defendant, that it ended in his favour, that there was no reasonable and probable cause, malice, and damage to him."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Contributory Negligence","q":"The doctrine of 'last opportunity' (last clear chance) in the law of negligence originated in:","o":["Ashby v. White (1703)","Davies v. Mann (1842)","Bird v. Jones (1845)","Butterfield v. Forrester (1809)"],"a":1,"e":"In the case of the donkey left fettered on the highway, the defendant who had the last opportunity of avoiding the accident was held liable. Butterfield v. Forrester established contributory negligence itself."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Property","q":"Under the Indian Easements Act, 1882, an easement is a right which the owner or occupier of land has:","o":["on the land of another, to take its produce or profit for sale","on the land of another, for the beneficial enjoyment of his own land","over movable property of another, for his own personal convenience","over his own land, for the benefit of any other third person at all"],"a":1,"e":"A right to extract produce from another's land is a profit à prendre, not an easement. Easements may be acquired by grant, prescription (20 years) or necessity."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Lease","q":"Under Section 107 of the Transfer of Property Act, a lease of immovable property from year to year, or for a term exceeding one year, can be made only by:","o":["a registered instrument","an oral agreement accompanied by delivery of possession","an unregistered deed signed by the lessor alone","a notarised affidavit by both the lessor and the lessee"],"a":0,"e":"Leases of immovable property from month to month or for less than a year may be made by an unregistered instrument or oral agreement with delivery of possession."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Marriage","q":"Under the Special Marriage Act, 1954, notice of the intended marriage is published by the Marriage Officer for:","o":["60 days","15 days","30 days","7 days"],"a":2,"e":"Objections may be raised during the 30-day period. Courts have debated whether mandatory publication of notice infringes privacy, since it exposes couples, especially in inter-faith marriages."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Adoption","q":"In Shabnam Hashmi v. Union of India (2014), the Supreme Court held that:","o":["only Hindus, Sikhs, Jains and Buddhists may adopt a child in India","personal laws bar adoption by Muslims even under the Juvenile Justice Act","the JJ Act enables any person, irrespective of religion, to adopt a child","adoption by non-Hindus is possible only after approval by the High Court"],"a":2,"e":"The Court held that the JJ Act is a secular enabling law, though it did not make adoption a fundamental right, leaving the question of a uniform adoption law to the legislature."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Definition of Law","q":"'Law is the body of principles recognised and applied by the State in the administration of justice' is the definition given by:","o":["Salmond","T.E. Holland","Rudolf von Jhering","John Chipman Gray"],"a":0,"e":"Salmond's definition focuses on the courts and the State's enforcement. Gray treated law as what judges declare, and Holland as a general rule of external human action enforced by a sovereign political authority."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Sociological School","q":"The concept of 'living law', distinct from the law found in State enactments, is associated with:","o":["Leon Duguit","Henry Maine","Eugen Ehrlich","Georg W.F. Hegel"],"a":2,"e":"Ehrlich, a founder of the sociology of law, argued that the centre of gravity of legal development lies in society itself and not in legislation or judicial decisions."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Anthropological School","q":"The movement of progressive societies 'from Status to Contract' is associated with:","o":["Max Weber","Henry Maine","Roscoe Pound","Montesquieu"],"a":1,"e":"Maine's Ancient Law studied the evolution of legal institutions through the historical-comparative method, and described the shift from fixed, family-based status to individual contractual relations."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Rights","q":"A right that is available against all persons in the world at large is known as:","o":["a positive right","a right in personam","a right in rem","an inchoate right"],"a":2,"e":"Rights in rem, such as ownership, correlate with a duty on everyone to refrain from interference. Rights in personam, such as contractual rights, are enforceable only against determinate persons."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Legal Personality","q":"In Shiromani Gurdwara Parbandhak Committee v. Som Nath Dass (2000), the Supreme Court held which of the following to be a juristic person?","o":["The Guru Granth Sahib","The Ramcharitmanas","The Bhagavad Gita","The Quran"],"a":0,"e":"The Court held the holy scripture to be a juristic person, given the faith's reverence for it as a living Guru, which allows it to hold property through its manager."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Legal Personality","q":"Mohd. Salim v. State of Uttarakhand (2017), in which the High Court declared the rivers Ganga and Yamuna to be legal persons, was:","o":["enacted into law by Parliament through a special statute","stayed by the Supreme Court","upheld by the Supreme Court with minor modifications","affirmed by a Constitution Bench of the Supreme Court"],"a":1,"e":"The Supreme Court stayed the order, partly because practical issues, such as liability for floods, were unresolved, as the Uttarakhand Government contended."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Maxims","q":"The maxim 'ignorantia juris non excusat' means:","o":["ignorance of fact is no excuse in any case","ignorance of the law is no excuse","a mistake of law excuses a first-time offender","one who knows the law cannot be punished"],"a":1,"e":"The rule ensures that persons cannot escape liability by pleading ignorance. Mistake of fact, in contrast, may be a defence under the general exceptions."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Maxims","q":"The maxim 'qui facit per alium facit per se' underlies the law of:","o":["adverse possession, as one who occupies another's land owns it","negotiable instruments, as a bearer may transfer by delivery","estoppel, as a person who speaks once cannot speak again","vicarious liability, as he who acts through another acts himself"],"a":3,"e":"The principal is liable for acts done by the agent within the scope of authority, and an employer for torts of employees committed in the course of employment."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Maxims","q":"The maxim 'caveat emptor' means:","o":["let the seller beware","let the court beware","let the principal beware","let the buyer beware"],"a":3,"e":"Section 16 of the Sale of Goods Act states the general rule that there is no implied condition as to quality or fitness, subject to exceptions such as sales by description and reliance on the seller's skill."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Maxims","q":"The maxim 'ex turpi causa non oritur actio' means:","o":["no action arises unless the defendant has been warned","no action arises from a base or illegal cause","no action lies where no damage has been caused","no action can be brought after the death of the plaintiff"],"a":1,"e":"Courts refuse to assist a claimant whose claim is founded on his own illegal or immoral conduct, as for example in agreements that are unlawful under Section 23 of the Contract Act."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Maxims","q":"The maxim 'nullum crimen sine lege' means:","o":["there is no crime without a law","there is no punishment without a trial","there is no law without a sovereign","there is no crime without punishment"],"a":0,"e":"It underlies Article 20(1) of the Constitution, which prohibits conviction under an ex post facto criminal law."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Maxims","q":"The maxim 'ei incumbit probatio qui dicit, non qui negat' means:","o":["an assertion is taken to be true unless the opposite party denies it","the court must itself prove whatever the parties have failed to prove","the burden of proof lies on the one who denies, not on one who asserts","the burden of proof lies on the one who asserts, not on one who denies"],"a":3,"e":"It is the basis of the general rule on burden of proof (Section 104 of the BSA), subject to statutory exceptions and presumptions."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"ICJ Advisory Opinions","q":"In its 1996 advisory opinion on the Legality of the Threat or Use of Nuclear Weapons, the ICJ concluded that:","o":["use of nuclear weapons is lawful only if used by a permanent member","there is no specific ban, but use would generally breach humanitarian law","use of nuclear weapons is specifically banned by a treaty in force today","use of nuclear weapons is lawful in every circumstance of an armed conflict"],"a":1,"e":"The Court could not conclude definitively whether the use would be lawful or unlawful in an extreme circumstance of self-defence, in which the very survival of a State would be at stake."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"ICJ Jurisdiction","q":"Under Article 36(2) of the ICJ Statute (the 'optional clause'), the Court has compulsory jurisdiction:","o":["over every dispute referred by the Security Council","in relation to any State accepting the same obligation by declaration","over disputes only between permanent members of the Security Council","over every State that is a member of the United Nations"],"a":1,"e":"States may attach reservations; India's declaration, for instance, excludes disputes with Commonwealth members, among other exceptions."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"United Nations","q":"Decisions of the UN Security Council on substantive (non-procedural) matters require:","o":["unanimity of all fifteen members of the Security Council, without exception","a simple majority of eight votes, regardless of the permanent members' views","a two-thirds majority of the whole General Assembly membership present","nine votes, including the concurring votes of the permanent members"],"a":3,"e":"Under Article 27(3) of the UN Charter, a negative vote of a permanent member is a veto, though in practice abstention is not treated as a veto."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Use of Force","q":"Which Article of the UN Charter preserves the inherent right of individual or collective self-defence against an armed attack?","o":["Article 2(4)","Article 51","Article 39","Article 42"],"a":1,"e":"Article 2(4) prohibits the threat or use of force, Article 39 empowers the Security Council to determine threats to peace, and Article 42 authorises enforcement action."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Climate Diplomacy","q":"The 30th Conference of the Parties (COP30) to the UNFCCC, in November 2025, was hosted in:","o":["Baku, Azerbaijan","Sharm el-Sheikh, Egypt","Belém, Brazil","Dubai, United Arab Emirates"],"a":2,"e":"COP29 was held in Baku (2024), COP28 in Dubai (2023) and COP27 in Sharm el-Sheikh (2022). (Verify the host of COP31 before the exam.)"},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Human Rights","q":"India's position on the UN Convention against Torture (CAT) is that it has:","o":["ratified it in 1997 with reservations on Article 20","not signed it, as Article 21 gives similar protection","acceded to it without making any reservation or declaration","signed it in 1997 but not ratified it"],"a":3,"e":"Ratification requires domestic legislation, and the Prevention of Torture Bill has not been enacted. Courts have nonetheless treated custodial torture as violating Article 21 (D.K. Basu)."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Trade Law","q":"The WTO Appellate Body has been unable to hear appeals since December 2019 because:","o":["the United States has blocked the appointment of its members","India has withdrawn from the WTO dispute settlement system","China has vetoed the appointment of new Appellate Body members","the General Council has voted to abolish the Appellate Body"],"a":0,"e":"With fewer than three members, the Appellate Body cannot form divisions. Appeals 'into the void' allow losing parties to avoid adoption of panel reports, which has prompted interim arrangements such as the MPIA."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Genocide Convention","q":"The case of The Gambia v. Myanmar before the ICJ was instituted under:","o":["the Convention on the Elimination of Racial Discrimination","the Genocide Convention, 1948","the Geneva Convention on the Treatment of Prisoners of War","the International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights"],"a":1,"e":"The Gambia has standing as a State party, since obligations under the Genocide Convention are owed erga omnes partes. The ICJ indicated provisional measures in January 2020."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Law of the Sea","q":"In the South China Sea Arbitration (Philippines v. China, 2016), the tribunal held that:","o":["the Spratly Islands each generate a full 200-nautical-mile EEZ zone","China's nine-dash line is lawful on the basis of its historic title","the tribunal lacked jurisdiction, since China refused to participate","China's nine-dash line claim to historic rights had no basis in UNCLOS"],"a":3,"e":"The tribunal, constituted under Annex VII of UNCLOS at the PCA, found that none of the features in the Spratly Islands is an 'island' entitled to an EEZ. China rejected the award."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Personality Rights","q":"Personality rights orders passed by the Delhi High Court (for example, in Anil Kapoor v. Simply Life India, 2023) protect a person's:","o":["name, image, voice and likeness against unauthorised commercial use","film scripts, but only when registered with the Copyright Office","inventions, but only when patented by the Controller of Patents","trade marks, but only when registered with the Trade Marks Registry"],"a":0,"e":"Courts have extended such protection against misuse through AI, deepfakes and merchandise, drawing on passing off, privacy under Article 21 and the right of publicity."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Copyright","q":"The author's 'moral rights' under Section 57 of the Copyright Act, 1957 include the right to:","o":["receive a royalty on every subsequent resale of the work","register the work as a trade mark under the 1999 Act","claim authorship and to restrain distortion of the work","sell the copyright for any term, without any statutory limit"],"a":2,"e":"These special rights subsist independently of the author's copyright, even after its assignment, and may be enforced by the author or the legal representatives."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Compulsory Licensing","q":"India's first compulsory licence under the Patents Act, granted in 2012 to Natco Pharma, concerned the drug:","o":["tenofovir (Viread), patented by Gilead","sorafenib tosylate (Nexavar), patented by Bayer","sofosbuvir (Sovaldi), patented by Gilead","imatinib mesylate (Glivec), patented by Novartis"],"a":1,"e":"The Controller granted the licence under Section 84 on grounds that reasonable requirements of the public were not satisfied, the drug was not available at an affordable price and the patent was not worked in India."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Intermediary Liability","q":"As read down in Shreya Singhal (2015), an intermediary loses its Section 79 safe harbour protection on 'actual knowledge' obtained through:","o":["a grievance filed by any user of the intermediary's platform","an automated alert generated by the platform's own algorithm","a court order or a notification of the appropriate Government","a notice sent by any private person who claims an offence"],"a":2,"e":"The reading prevents intermediaries from deciding the legality of content on their own, which would otherwise cause over-removal and chill free speech."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Competition Law","q":"Under Section 3(3) of the Competition Act, 2002, horizontal agreements such as price fixing and bid rigging among competitors are:","o":["conclusively held to be unlawful, with no scope for rebuttal by the parties","lawful, unless the CCI proves that actual harm was caused to consumers","presumed to have appreciable adverse effect on competition, unless rebutted","exempt from scrutiny, if they are entered into by small enterprises only"],"a":2,"e":"The rebuttable presumption relates to cartels, while vertical agreements are tested under the rule of reason in Section 3(4)."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Insolvency","q":"Section 29A of the Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code, 2016 debars from submitting a resolution plan:","o":["all foreign companies and non-resident Indians, as a class of persons","all promoters of the corporate debtor, in each and every circumstance","all persons who have earlier worked in the same line of industry","wilful defaulters and persons with unpaid accounts classified as NPAs"],"a":3,"e":"Upheld in Swiss Ribbons v. Union of India (2019) and ArcelorMittal v. Satish Kumar Gupta (2019), the section prevents errant promoters from regaining control of their companies at a discount."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Company Law","q":"Under Section 149(3) of the Companies Act, 2013, every company must have at least one director who has stayed in India for a total of not less than:","o":["182 days in the previous calendar year","240 days in the previous financial year","120 days in the previous calendar year","90 days in the previous financial year"],"a":0,"e":"This is the 'resident director' requirement. A newly incorporated company must satisfy it proportionately in the financial year of incorporation."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Banking Law","q":"Under the SARFAESI Act, 2002, a secured creditor can enforce a security interest:","o":["only after prior approval from the Debts Recovery Tribunal concerned","without court or tribunal intervention, after notice under Section 13(2)","only through a decree of the civil court followed by its execution","only after a specific prior sanction by the Reserve Bank of India"],"a":1,"e":"A borrower aggrieved by a measure may appeal to the DRT under Section 17. Mardia Chemicals v. Union of India (2004) upheld the Act, subject to pre-deposit modifications."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"IT Rules","q":"Under the Information Technology (Intermediary Guidelines and Digital Media Ethics Code) Rules, 2021, a 'significant social media intermediary' is one with registered users of at least:","o":["50 lakh (5 million)","10 lakh (1 million)","25 lakh (2.5 million)","1 crore (10 million)"],"a":0,"e":"Such intermediaries must appoint a Chief Compliance Officer, a nodal contact person and a resident grievance officer, and enable identification of the first originator of a message in certain cases."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Data Protection","q":"The DPDP Rules, 2025, notified in November 2025, provide for phased commencement, under which the main obligations on Data Fiduciaries apply after:","o":["36 months from notification","6 months from notification","3 months from notification","18 months from notification"],"a":3,"e":"The Data Protection Board provisions took effect early, while consent manager provisions follow after 12 months. (Verify the commencement timeline in the notification.)"},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Arbitration","q":"In Amazon.com NV Investment Holdings v. Future Retail Ltd. (2021), the Supreme Court held that an emergency arbitrator's award is:","o":["enforceable only if both the parties agree to its enforcement later","enforceable only after a civil court has granted a decree upon it","unenforceable, since an emergency arbitrator is not a tribunal under the Act","enforceable as an order of the arbitral tribunal under Section 17(1)"],"a":3,"e":"The Court held that party autonomy allows institutional rules, such as the SIAC Rules, to provide for emergency arbitration, and orders under it can be enforced under Section 17(2)."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Estoppel","q":"The doctrine of promissory estoppel traces to which English case?","o":["Central London Property Trust v. High Trees House (1947)","Carlill v. Carbolic Smoke Ball Company (1893), on offers","Jorden v. Money (1854), on the estoppel by representation","Tweddle v. Atkinson (1861), on the rule of privity of contract"],"a":0,"e":"Lord Denning's judgment in High Trees developed the principle that a promise intended to be relied upon, and acted upon, is binding. In India, Motilal Padampat Sugar Mills v. State of U.P. (1979) applied it against the Government."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Proportionality","q":"In Om Kumar v. Union of India (2001), the Supreme Court held that the proportionality test applies where:","o":["the decision concerns a purely commercial transaction of the State","fundamental rights are affected, while Wednesbury applies otherwise","the decision is made by a purely private body and not by the State","the decision is of a legislative nature in every circumstance of law"],"a":1,"e":"The Court distinguished between review of administrative action affecting fundamental freedoms, where the court may test the choice of the penalty, and a review of discretion that does not affect such freedoms."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Lokpal","q":"Under the Lokpal and Lokayuktas Act, 2013, the Lokpal's jurisdiction over the Prime Minister excludes allegations relating to:","o":["election disputes, funds of political parties and State governments","appointments of judges, constitutional bodies and State Governors","international relations, security, public order, atomic energy and space","taxation, trade and commerce, and the allocation of the Union budget"],"a":2,"e":"Inquiry against the Prime Minister must be held in camera and needs approval of at least two-thirds of the Lokpal's full bench."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"NGT","q":"The National Green Tribunal does NOT have jurisdiction over disputes arising under which of the following laws?","o":["The Water (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act, 1974","The Biological Diversity Act, 2002","The Wild Life (Protection) Act, 1972","The Air (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act, 1981"],"a":2,"e":"Schedule I to the NGT Act covers seven laws: the Water Act, the Water Cess Act, the Forest (Conservation) Act, the Air Act, the Environment (Protection) Act, the Public Liability Insurance Act and the Biological Diversity Act. The Wild Life Act and the Indian Forest Act are not included."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Climate Commitments","q":"India's declared target at COP26, Glasgow (2021), for achieving net-zero emissions is the year:","o":["2070","2060","2050","2040"],"a":0,"e":"Prime Minister Modi announced the 'Panchamrit' commitments, including 500 GW non-fossil capacity by 2030 and net-zero by 2070."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Maternity","q":"Under the Maternity Benefit Act, 1961, as amended in 2017, a woman with fewer than two surviving children is entitled to paid maternity leave of:","o":["18 weeks","24 weeks","26 weeks","12 weeks"],"a":2,"e":"The leave is 12 weeks for a woman with two or more surviving children. The amendment also mandates creches in establishments with 50 or more employees."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"POSH Act","q":"Under the Sexual Harassment of Women at Workplace Act, 2013, an employer must constitute an Internal Committee at each office or branch having at least:","o":["50 employees","20 employees","5 employees","10 employees"],"a":3,"e":"Where there are fewer than ten employees, a Local Committee at the district level handles complaints. The Internal Committee must have a senior woman as Presiding Officer and an external member."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Child Labour","q":"The Child and Adolescent Labour (Prohibition and Regulation) Amendment Act, 2016 prohibits employment of children below 14 years in all occupations, subject to an exception for:","o":["working in factories, if both parents have given their written consent","working in mines, if the work is done only during school holidays","family enterprises after school and audio-visual entertainment work","working in domestic service, if the employer is a close relative"],"a":2,"e":"Adolescents aged 14 to 18 are barred from hazardous occupations and processes. Article 24 of the Constitution prohibits employment of children below 14 in hazardous work."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Food Security","q":"The National Food Security Act, 2013 covers up to what proportion of the rural and urban populations as priority households?","o":["50% rural and 25% urban","75% rural and 50% urban","60% rural and 40% urban","90% rural and 75% urban"],"a":1,"e":"Entitled persons get 5 kg of foodgrains per person per month at subsidised prices. The Act recognises nutrition support for pregnant women and children, following PUCL v. Union of India (2001) on the right to food."},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Labour Law","q":"The four Labour Codes were brought into force by the Government of India with effect from:","o":["26 January 2025","21 November 2025","15 August 2025","1 April 2020"],"a":1,"e":"The Codes on Wages, Industrial Relations, Social Security and Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions consolidate 29 central labour laws. (Verify the notification before the exam.)"},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Constitutional Offices","q":"Who took charge as the Chief Election Commissioner of India in February 2025?","o":["Rajiv Kumar","Sunil Arora","Gyanesh Kumar","Sushil Chandra"],"a":2,"e":"Gyanesh Kumar succeeded Rajiv Kumar and was appointed under the 2023 Act. (Verify before the exam.)"},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Waqf Act","q":"In its interim order of September 2025 on challenges to the Waqf (Amendment) Act, 2025, the Supreme Court:","o":["stayed the entire Act until the petitions are finally heard and decided","refused to stay any provision, observing that the whole Act is valid","struck down the whole Act as violative of Articles 25 and 26 itself","stayed only some provisions, like the five-year practising Muslim rule"],"a":3,"e":"The Court also capped non-Muslim members in the Waqf bodies, and stayed the Collector's power to decide whether a property is government land, pending final decision. (Verify the details.)"},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Extradition","q":"In April 2025, the United States extradited Tahawwur Rana to India in connection with:","o":["the 2008 Mumbai (26/11) terror attacks","the 2001 attack on the Indian Parliament","the 1993 serial bomb blasts in Mumbai","the 2019 Pulwama attack on CRPF convoy"],"a":0,"e":"Rana, a Pakistani-origin Canadian citizen, was extradited after the US courts upheld the request. Under the rule of speciality, he can be tried only for the offences for which extradition was granted."},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Judicial Accountability","q":"The 'in-house procedure' for dealing with complaints against sitting judges of the higher judiciary was evolved in:","o":["C. Ravichandran Iyer v. Justice A.M. Bhattacharjee (1995)","K. Veeraswami v. Union of India (1991)","L. Chandra Kumar v. Union of India (1997)","Supreme Court Advocates-on-Record Association v. Union of India (2015)"],"a":0,"e":"The Court recognised an in-house mechanism led by the Chief Justice, short of impeachment, which was adopted in 1999 and has been in the news for complaints involving judges. A judge can formally be removed only through Article 124(4)."},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Parliamentary Committees","q":"Who chairs the Joint Parliamentary Committee examining the Constitution (129th Amendment) Bill, 2024 on simultaneous elections?","o":["Ravi Shankar Prasad","P.P. Chaudhary","Anurag Thakur","Jagdambika Pal"],"a":1,"e":"Jagdambika Pal chaired the JPC on the Waqf (Amendment) Bill. (Verify the current membership before the exam.)"},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Postal Law","q":"The Post Office Act, 2023 repealed which colonial-era law?","o":["The Indian Post Office Act, 1898","The Indian Railways Act, 1890","The Indian Wireless Telegraphy Act, 1933","The Indian Telegraph Act, 1885"],"a":0,"e":"The new Act removes the Government's exclusive privilege on letters, but retains powers of interception in specified circumstances."},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Decriminalisation","q":"The Jan Vishwas (Amendment of Provisions) Act, 2023, which decriminalised minor offences to improve ease of living and doing business, amended how many Central Acts?","o":["64","28","42","19"],"a":2,"e":"The Act, passed in August 2023, covers laws administered by 19 ministries, replacing imprisonment with penalties for minor, technical defaults."}],"b":[{"id":"S1","m":25,"w":450,"q":"The Supreme Court's 2024 rulings in Sita Soren (parliamentary privilege) and the Electoral Bonds case reflect a renewed focus on probity in public life. Discuss how far the courts can, within the separation of powers, police corruption in electoral and legislative processes.","o":["Constitutional text: Articles 105 and 194 (privileges), Article 19(1)(a) (voters' right to know), Article 14 (arbitrariness).","Sita Soren (2024) overruling P.V. Narasimha Rao: bribery is not protected by privilege.","ADR v. Union of India (2024): anonymity in political funding versus the right to information.","Counter-arguments: judicial overreach, institutional comity, role of Parliament and the Election Commission.","Conclusion: courts as guardians of democratic integrity, with legislative reform needed on funding transparency and the anti-defection regime."]},{"id":"S2","m":25,"w":450,"q":"The Labour Codes came into force in November 2025, consolidating 29 central labour laws. Critically evaluate whether they strike a fair balance between ease of doing business and protection of workers' rights, with reference to Articles 14, 21, 38, 39 and 43.","o":["Background: consolidation into four Codes (Wages, Industrial Relations, Social Security, OSH).","Pro-business features: higher thresholds for retrenchment and closure approval, fixed-term employment, simplified compliance.","Worker-protection features: universal minimum wage, social security for gig and platform workers, gender-neutral provisions.","Concerns: reduced bargaining power, strike notice conditions, state-level variation, enforcement capacity.","Constitutional lens: Directive Principles (Arts 38, 39, 41, 42, 43), Bangalore Water Supply, and the reasonableness test; conclusion with suggested safeguards."]}]};
